## One token, then the next

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 only does one thing: given some text, it gives a chance to every possible next token.
Writing a paragraph is a loop around that: run the model, pick a token, add it to the end, and
repeat. Every chat app writes this way. Try it: each press runs the model once. Click any word in
the list to pick it instead of the model's favorite.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 only ever does one thing: given some text, it gives a probability for every possible next
token. That's the whole stack from Part IV, run once. Writing a paragraph is just a loop around it:

1. run the model on the text so far,
2. pick a token from its probabilities,
3. add that token to the end of the text,
4. repeat.

Every model in a chat app writes this way. Try it yourself: each press runs the full model once.
Click any word in the list to pick it instead of the model's favorite.

</At>

<GlassBoxGenerator
	title="Glass-box generator"
	presets={[{ trace: 'the-cat-sat', label: 'The cat sat on the' }]}
	branches={['the-cat-sat-bed', 'the-cat-sat-couch', 'the-cat-sat-ground', 'the-cat-sat-edge']}
/>

<At levels="simple">

Keep pressing *Add the most likely* and GPT-2 writes "The cat sat on the floor, and the cat was
still asleep." Go back and pick " bed" instead, and it gets stuck saying the same thing over and
over. Picking at random, weighted by the chances, mostly avoids loops like this.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Keep pressing *Add the most likely* and GPT-2 writes "The cat sat on the floor, and the cat was
still asleep." Go back and pick " bed" instead, and it gets stuck: ", and the cat sat on the bed, and
the cat sat on the bed…". Always taking the single most likely token often falls into loops like
this. Picking at random, weighted by the probabilities (the <GlossaryPopover term="temperature">temperature</GlossaryPopover> spinner from <ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />), mostly
avoids them, which is one reason most text generators sample instead.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Greedy isn't the likeliest text

Written out, greedy decoding takes the top token at each step, and sampling draws one from the
tempered probabilities $P_T$:

$$
x_{t+1} = \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{x} P(x \mid x_1, \dots, x_t) \qquad \text{or} \qquad x_{t+1} \sim P_T(\,\cdot \mid x_1, \dots, x_t)
$$

where $x_1, \dots, x_t$ is the text so far and $\sim$ means "drawn at random from". The best
token at each step doesn't add up to the best text, because a text's chance is the product of its
tokens' chances. After *The cat sat on the*, greedy takes " floor" (7.6%), and then a comma (26%):
about 2% for the pair. " edge" starts lower (4.8%), but " of" follows it 96% of the time, so
" edge of" has about 4.6%, more than twice as likely. Beam search keeps several partial texts at
each step to find likelier ones, but the likeliest texts are often the dullest and most
repetitive, which is part of why open-ended writing samples instead.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>

## No going back

<At levels="simple">

Each token is chosen once, and everything after it builds on it. The model doesn't plan ahead or
go back to fix a word. And every new token means one more full run of the model.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Each token is chosen once, and everything after it is built on top. The model doesn't plan a
sentence, write a draft, or go back and fix an earlier word. If it picks " bed", it has to make the
best of " bed". And each new token costs a full run through all 124 million of GPT-2's weights, so
a 500-token answer means 500 runs.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

A run costs about two operations per weight, a multiply and an add, so GPT-2 small needs about
250 million operations per token, and about 124 billion for those 500 tokens.<Cite id="kaplan2020" />
Rerunning the model on the whole text for every token would cost far more, since the text grows
by one token each time. Real systems keep each earlier token's keys and values from attention and
only run the newest token through the model; <ChapterRef to="caching" /> shows how.<Cite id="pope2022" />

</At>

## Knowing when to stop

<At levels="simple">

The loop has to stop somehow. It stops when the model picks a special "end of text" token, or
when it reaches a length limit it was given.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The loop has to end somehow. GPT-2's vocabulary includes a special token, `<|endoftext|>` (number
50,256), that means "end of text". When the model picks it, the program stops.<Cite id="gpt2-config" />
Otherwise, the program stops at a length limit it was given. Chat models work the same way
with their own end-of-turn token.

</At>

## Run it live

Everything above is recorded. To type your own beginning, pick any word, and change the temperature,
choose the Small model tier on the Models page, linked from the top bar. It downloads a compressed
copy of GPT-2 small, with the tier's three other small models and the code that runs them (about
419 MB), and runs it on your device. Nothing you type leaves your browser.

<RecapCard>

- A model only predicts the next token. Generating text is a loop: predict, pick, append, repeat.
- Each token costs one full run of the model, and once picked it is never revised.
- Always taking the top token can loop; sampling with a temperature mostly avoids that.
- Generation stops at a special end token or a length limit.

<At levels="deep">

- Greedy takes the $\operatorname{arg\,max}$ at each step, which isn't the likeliest whole text:
  " edge of" (4.6%) beats " floor," (2%).

</At>
</RecapCard>

